# Swiper carousels

Carousels use the React components from `swiper/react` plus `import 'swiper/css'` at the top of the component file. Everything below is verified against the installed package (`node_modules/swiper/swiper.css`, `node_modules/swiper/shared/swiper-core.mjs`) and <https://swiperjs.com/swiper-api>.

## What the library's CSS already sets

```css
.swiper-wrapper {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

.swiper-slide {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  flex-shrink: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

/* only when the `autoHeight` param is on */
.swiper-autoheight,
.swiper-autoheight .swiper-slide {
  height: auto;
}
.swiper-autoheight .swiper-wrapper {
  align-items: flex-start;
}
```

The container (`.swiper`) has no height of its own, so the `height: 100%` chain — container → wrapper → slide — is indefinite. Percentage heights inside a slide therefore resolve to `auto`.

Class names Swiper adds at runtime and that are safe to style: `swiper-slide-active`, `swiper-slide-next`, `swiper-slide-prev`, `swiper-slide-visible`, `swiper-slide-fully-visible`. Do **not** rename them through `slideClass` / `slideActiveClass` — those params are documented as unsupported in the React components.

## Slide width under `slidesPerView: 'auto'`

With `slidesPerView: 'auto'` Swiper reads each slide's **computed width** (`getComputedStyle(slide).width` plus padding/margin) to build the snap grid. The slide must therefore carry a real width in CSS:

```scss
// good — explicit track item width
&_item {
  width: 336px;
}

// good — shrink-wrap a fixed-width card
&_item {
  width: fit-content;
}

// wrong — a block-level slide with `auto` fills the whole track
&_item {
  width: auto;
}
```

`width: auto` on a block box means "fill the containing block", so every slide measures the full track width and only one slide is ever visible.

## Equal-height slides

Give the slide `height: auto` and let the flex wrapper stretch it; the card then fills the slide:

```scss
// track item
&_item {
  width: 336px;
  height: auto;
}
```

```scss
// card root
.vertical {
  height: 100%;
}
```

Never turn a slide into a `display: flex` / `display: grid` container to achieve this. Grid and flex size their children by intrinsic width, and cards whose media is absolutely positioned (galleries with `next/image` `fill`) have almost no intrinsic width — the card collapses to its text's min-content width. Block-level slides let the card fill instead.

`autoHeight: true` is the opposite feature: the wrapper shrinks to the **active** slide's height. Use it only for single-slide-per-view carousels with unequal content.

## Params

- `spaceBetween` is applied by Swiper as a margin on each slide. Never add your own `margin` to slides — the docs warn navigation breaks.
- `slidesOffsetBefore` / `slidesOffsetAfter` add px padding to the start/end of the track; prefer them over container padding, which clips the drag area.
- `breakpoints` may only change params that do not alter layout logic — `slidesPerView`, `slidesPerGroup`, `spaceBetween`, `grid.rows`, and the offsets. `loop` and `effect` are ignored there.
- `rewind` and `loop` are mutually exclusive.
- Import only the modules used (`import { A11y } from 'swiper/modules'`) and pass them via `modules={[...]}`; `swiper/css` alone covers the core styles, module styles are separate imports.

